        "excerpts": [
          "i Way of Life &amp; Culture Kiribati has developed a distinct way of life shaped by its geography – 32 low-lying atolls and one raised coral island scattered across vast stretches of the Pacific Ocean. Although part of the wider Pacific family, Kiribati maintains a unique culture, language, and identity that have been preserved through generations, largely thanks to its isolation and strong community values. As a traveller, taking time to learn a few local words and observe cultural customs is a",
          ", toddy cutting, cultivation and harvesting of food crops (coconut, pandanus, breadfruit and taro), weaving mats, making roof thatch, rolling string, making fish traps and hooks, amongst others. Most of these activities are performed by the adult members of the family, whilst the younger members collect firewood, fetch water, clean the dishes, clean the compound, and assist the adult members in doing parts of their chores. Today, traditional subsistence living in Kiribati has become increasingly",
          "community routines. Sustenance comes from the bounty of the sea and the land. Coconuts, breadfruit, pandanus, and taro provide nourishment and continuity for island life. The traditional subsistence economy of Kiribati is closely reflected in the social organisation of family life. Families come together to form clans, or te kaainga, which operate under shared customs, responsibilities, and mutual support. Daily tasks are traditionally divided by gender: men fish, collect toddy, and build, while",
          "tence activities include fishing, toddy cutting, cultivation and harvesting of food crops (coconut, pandanus, breadfruit and taro), weaving mats, making roof thatch, rolling string, making fish traps and hooks, amongst others. Most of these activities are performed by the adult members of the family, whilst the younger members collect firewood, fetch water, clean the dishes, clean the compound, and assist the adult members in doing parts of their chores. Today, traditional subsistence living in ",
          "o play an active role in fishing like gathering shellfish, net fishing or hunting sand worms on the lagoon or reef flats, while men venture into the open ocean to fish. There is a clear status of ranking in each household. Family households are usually led by the oldest male, unless he is too elderly or too sick to be active, in which case the next oldest male then leads the family. It is important for the survival of the group that each member fulfils the roles and responsibilities assigned to "
        ]
      },
      {
        "url": "https://kiribatitourism.gov.ki/gilbert-islands",
        "http_status": 200,
        "excerpts": [
          "eauty &amp; Literary Ties Abemama offers a more secluded and idyllic escape, known for its tranquil lagoon and historical connections. Robert Louis Stevenson: The famous author of Treasure Island once called Abemama home, drawing inspiration from its serene beauty and peaceful environment. Traditional Living: Abemama is an excellent place to witness the simplicity of the traditional I-Kiribati lifestyle, including traditional canoe building and fishing methods. Cultural Immersion &amp; Community"
        ]
